// src/kernel/core/drivers/i2c/lpss_probe.pdx — R30.M5-003 (#1072)
//
// LPSS I²C controller PCI probe, BAR discovery, and the controller
// table the rest of R30.M5 hangs off.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// IDENTIFICATION: NO DEVICE-ID TABLE
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This driver does not carry a list of PCI device IDs, and the omission
// is the design.
//
// A device-ID table is a claim about which silicon exists. It is a claim
// that is wrong the moment a machine ships that the table's author had
// not seen — and the failure it produces is the worst-shaped one
// available: the controller is present, healthy, and simply not probed.
// Nothing errors. The touchpad is dead, the sensor hub is absent, and
// the log says nothing at all, because nothing happened. A T14 G4's
// device IDs will not match a different Raptor Lake SKU, let alone a
// Meteor Lake one, and every generation would need an entry added by
// someone who owned that machine.
//
// So identification is two-stage, and neither stage is a list:
//
//   STAGE 1 — CANDIDATE FILTER, by PCI class. Cheap, needs no MMIO,
//   and admits a superset:
//
//       class 0x0C subclass 0x80   Serial bus controller, other
//       class 0x11 subclass 0x80   Signal processing controller, other
//
//   Both appear in the wild for LPSS blocks depending on how the
//   platform's firmware configured the PCH, so both are candidates.
//   The filter is deliberately loose: a false candidate costs one
//   register read, and a missed one costs a dead device.
//
//   STAGE 2 — CONFIRMATION, by the IP's own identity register.
//   Every Synopsys DW_apb_i2c instance ever synthesised reads back
//       IC_COMP_TYPE (BAR0 + 0xFC) == 0x44570140
//   regardless of vendor, wrapper, synthesis parameters or SoC
//   generation. That constant is the actual, authoritative answer to
//   "is this a DesignWare I²C core", and it comes from the part rather
//   than from a table someone maintained.
//
// Stage 2 lives in dw_init.pdx (`dw_i2c_identify`) because it needs the
// window capability and the reset release first; this file's job ends
// at a CANDIDATE. A candidate that fails stage 2 is moved to
// LPSS_I2C_STATE_REJECTED and logged — it is never silently dropped,
// because "we found something at this BDF and it was not what we
// expected" is exactly the sentence a person debugging a new machine
// needs to read.
//
// The one vendor-specific fact used anywhere is the Intel wrapper's
// reset register, and it is gated on VID == 0x8086 and recorded in the
// row rather than assumed. A non-Intel DesignWare instance on PCI skips
// the reset release and goes straight to stage 2.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE BAR IS REACHED THROUGH A CAPABILITY
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The probe learns BAR0's physical base and length. It does not hand
// that address to anyone. `lpss_i2c_bind_window` mints a
// `KIND_OP_REGION` root over the caller's `KIND_MEMORY` authority,
// covering exactly the recorded {base, len}, and records the resulting
// CAPABILITY SLOT in the row. Everything downstream — bring-up,
// transfers, recovery — reaches registers only through
// `dw_io_read32` / `dw_io_write32`, which resolve that slot per access.
//
// The base and length passed to the mint are the row's own, INHERITED
// from what the probe measured, not arguments. That is R30.M4-003's
// discipline and #1070's: a field that identifies which physical thing
// an authority is about must come from the authority that was checked.
// If a caller could supply the base, a holder of memory authority could
// mint itself a window over any physical address at all and call it an
// I²C controller — which is the whole class of bug `KIND_OP_REGION`
// exists to remove, re-introduced by its own client.
//
// Address space is `OPREG_SPACE_PCI_BAR` (0x06), which
// `opregion_space_base_kind` classifies as memory-like and therefore
// requires a `KIND_MEMORY` parent holding `RIGHT_MINT`.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SIZING A BAR CORRECTLY
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `pci_bar_size` works by writing all-ones to the BAR and reading back
// which bits stuck. While that write is in place the BAR decodes a
// window at a garbage address, so the function's contract is that the
// caller disables the memory decoder first. Nothing in the tree had
// done that; `lpss_i2c_bar0` does, by clearing COMMAND bit 1 across the
// sizing and restoring the original word afterwards. Skipping it is not
// a theoretical risk: an enabled decoder pointed at 0xFFFFF000 during
// the probe overlaps whatever else is up there.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CONTROLLER ROW — `_lpss_i2c_ctrl[idx]`, 64 bytes
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   [+0]  u64  hdr
//               [7:0]   in_use      (0 = free, 1 = allocated)
//               [15:8]  bus
//               [23:16] dev
//               [31:24] function
//               [47:32] segment
//               [55:48] intel_wrapper (1 = VID 0x8086, private space
//                                      at BAR0+0x200 is present)
//   [+8]  u64  bar0_pa    — physical base measured at probe
//   [+16] u64  bar0_len   — window length granted to the capability
//   [+24] u64  window_slot — cap slot holding the KIND_OP_REGION, or 0
//   [+32] u64  bus_row    — the KIND_I2C_BUS row this controller backs,
//                           or LPSS_I2C_NO_BUS_ROW. This is the join
//                           between the capability world and the
//                           hardware world, and it is a ROW id and not
//                           a cap slot for #1070's reason: a slot can
//                           later hold a different bus, a live row id
//                           cannot.
//   [+40] u64  state      — LPSS_I2C_STATE_*
//   [+48] u64  ic_clk_hz  — recorded at bring-up, 0 until then
//   [+56] u64  health
//               [7:0]  wedged        (latched; see dw_xfer.pdx)
//               [15:8] recover_fails (consecutive)
//
// The table has exactly one writer object, asserted by `objdump -r` in
// `tools/build.sh`. That is why the health word and the state have
// exported mutators rather than being poked from dw_xfer.o — the same
// device, and for the same reason, as `i2c_bus_note_slave_added`.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// paideia-as posture
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   G1: sized stores (`mov_d` / `mov_w` / `mov_b`).
//   G4: full-register compares.
//   Reserved-label discipline: labels prefixed `lpi_`.

module LpssProbe = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Candidate filter
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let LPSS_CLASS_SERIAL_BUS : u64 = 0x0C
  pub let LPSS_CLASS_SIGNAL     : u64 = 0x11
  pub let LPSS_SUBCLASS_OTHER   : u64 = 0x80
  pub let LPSS_VENDOR_INTEL     : u64 = 0x8086

  // ==========================================================================
  // Table geometry
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let LPSS_I2C_MAX_CTRL   : u64 = 8
  pub let LPSS_I2C_ROW_BYTES  : u64 = 64
  pub let LPSS_I2C_NO_BUS_ROW : u64 = 0xFFFF
  pub let LPSS_I2C_NOT_FOUND  : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // States
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_FREE       : u64 = 0
  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_CANDIDATE  : u64 = 1   // class matched, BAR sized
  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_WINDOWED   : u64 = 2   // window capability bound
  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_IDENTIFIED : u64 = 3   // IC_COMP_TYPE confirmed
  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_READY      : u64 = 4   // brought up, enabled
  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_REJECTED   : u64 = 5   // IC_COMP_TYPE mismatch

  // ==========================================================================
  // Failure taxonomy — band 0xFFFFFF40..0xFFFFFF5F
  //
  // Disjoint from DW_IO_* (0xFFFFFF30..3F), I2C_XFER_* (0xFFFFFF60..6F),
  // I2C_SLAVE_* (0xFFFFFF70..7F), I2C_BUS_* (0xFFFFFF83..8F),
  // ACPI_EVT_* (0xFFFFFF90..9F), DRV_RESTART_* (0xFFFFFFB5..BE),
  // OPREG_* (0xFFFFFFC2..CF), DMA_* (0xFFFFFFD5..DF),
  // MSIX_* (0xFFFFFFE7..EF) and HW_INT_* (0xFFFFFFF7..FF).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let LPSS_I2C_OK                     : u64 = 0
  pub let LPSS_I2C_PROBE_ENOSPC           : u64 = 0xFFFFFF5F
  pub let LPSS_I2C_PROBE_BAD_IDX          : u64 = 0xFFFFFF5E
  pub let LPSS_I2C_PROBE_NO_BAR           : u64 = 0xFFFFFF5D
  pub let LPSS_I2C_PROBE_NOT_DESIGNWARE   : u64 = 0xFFFFFF5C
  pub let LPSS_I2C_WIN_BAD_PARENT         : u64 = 0xFFFFFF5B
  pub let LPSS_I2C_WIN_ALREADY            : u64 = 0xFFFFFF5A
  pub let LPSS_I2C_WIN_UNBOUND            : u64 = 0xFFFFFF59
  pub let LPSS_I2C_INIT_CLK_UNKNOWN       : u64 = 0xFFFFFF58
  pub let LPSS_I2C_INIT_SPEED_UNSUPPORTED : u64 = 0xFFFFFF57
  pub let LPSS_I2C_INIT_DIVIDER_RANGE     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF56
  pub let LPSS_I2C_INIT_DISABLE_TIMEOUT   : u64 = 0xFFFFFF55
  pub let LPSS_I2C_INIT_ENABLE_TIMEOUT    : u64 = 0xFFFFFF54
  pub let LPSS_I2C_INIT_NOT_IDENTIFIED    : u64 = 0xFFFFFF53
  pub let LPSS_I2C_BUS_ATTACH_BAD_ROW     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF52
  pub let LPSS_I2C_BUS_ATTACH_ALREADY     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF51
  pub let LPSS_I2C_IO_FAULT               : u64 = 0xFFFFFF50
  pub let LPSS_I2C_RECOVER_TIMEOUT        : u64 = 0xFFFFFF4F
  pub let LPSS_I2C_RECOVER_FAILED         : u64 = 0xFFFFFF4E
  pub let LPSS_I2C_NOT_WEDGED             : u64 = 0xFFFFFF4D
  pub let LPSS_I2C_SMBUS_UNSUPPORTED      : u64 = 0xFFFFFF4C
  pub let LPSS_I2C_ABORT_OTHER            : u64 = 0xFFFFFF4B
  pub let LPSS_I2C_STATE_BAD              : u64 = 0xFFFFFF4A
  pub let LPSS_I2C_IDENTIFY_ALREADY       : u64 = 0xFFFFFF49

  // Consecutive failed recoveries before the bus is declared wedged.
  pub let LPSS_I2C_RECOVER_MAX_FAILS      : u64 = 3

  // ==========================================================================
  // Storage. Confined to this object; tools/build.sh asserts it.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _lpss_i2c_ctrl  : [u64; 64] = uninit @align(64)
  pub let mut _lpss_i2c_count : u64 = 0

  // Log tags.
  pub let lpi_tag_probe  : [u8; 16] = "LPSS I2C PROBE\0"
  pub let lpi_tag_reject : [u8; 17] = "LPSS I2C REJECT\0"
  pub let lpi_k_n        : [u8; 2]  = "n\0"
  pub let lpi_k_bdf      : [u8; 4]  = "bdf\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_table_reset() -> ()
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_table_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): return the controller table to empty -- all 64 qwords zeroed and the count cleared. Zeroing the whole row and not merely the in_use byte is the scrub discipline #1070 established for the bus and slave tables, and it matters for the same reason: a freed row that kept its bar0_pa would let a later bind_window mint a window over an address the current probe never measured. Leaf; counted loop. Labels prefixed lpi_tr_.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_ctrl];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      lpi_tr_clear:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 64;
        jb  lpi_tr_clear;
        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_count];
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_count : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): number of controller rows allocated by the last probe. Leaf load.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_count];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_row(idx) -> address of the row, or 0 when idx is out of
  // range or the row is free.
  //
  // The single place row address arithmetic happens.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_row : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): resolve a controller index to the address of its 64-byte row, or 0 for an out-of-range index or a free row. Every accessor and mutator in this file goes through it, so the bounds check and the liveness check exist once. Returning an ADDRESS rather than a row id is safe here and not in the capability kinds, because this table is confined to this object -- no other object can hold the pointer, since no other object may relocate against the symbol. Leaf. Labels prefixed lpi_row_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        jae lpi_row_none;
        mov rax, rdi;
        shl rax, 6;                      // * 64
        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_ctrl];
        add rax, r10;
        mov r11, [rax + 0];
        and r11, 0xFF;
        cmp r11, 0;
        je  lpi_row_none;
        ret;
      lpi_row_none:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // Row field readers
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_bdf : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the controller's BDF in bdf_pack form -- fn[7:0] | dev[15:8] | bus[23:16] | seg[39:24] -- reassembled from the row header. bdf_pack form specifically, and not the 16-bit wire form the PCI enumerator's RPC uses, because this is the value a KIND_DEVICE descriptor's target_ptr carries and therefore the value kind_i2c_bus.pdx inherits as controller_id. Two encodings of the same fact already coexist in the tree; producing the one the capability system uses is what lets a caller match a controller to its bus without a third. Returns 0 for a dead row. One push (rbx) for the nested call. Labels prefixed lpi_bdf_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_bdf_out;
        mov rbx, [rax + 0];

        mov rax, rbx;
        shr rax, 24;
        and rax, 0xFF;                   // fn
        mov r10, rbx;
        shr r10, 16;
        and r10, 0xFF;
        shl r10, 8;                      // dev << 8
        or  rax, r10;
        mov r10, rbx;
        shr r10, 8;
        and r10, 0xFF;
        shl r10, 16;                     // bus << 16
        or  rax, r10;
        mov r10, rbx;
        shr r10, 32;
        mov r11, 0xFFFF;
        and r10, r11;
        shl r10, 24;                     // seg << 24
        or  rax, r10;

      lpi_bdf_out:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_intel : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): 1 iff this controller sits behind an Intel LPSS wrapper, i.e. the PCI vendor was 0x8086 and the private register bank at BAR0+0x200 exists. Recorded at probe rather than assumed, so the one vendor-specific write this driver performs -- the reset release -- is gated on a measured fact. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_intel_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_intel_out;
        mov r10, [rax + 0];
        shr r10, 48;
        and r10, 0xFF;
        mov rax, r10;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_intel_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_bar_base : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the BAR0 physical base measured at probe. Read by lpss_i2c_bind_window and by the witness; NOT read by any access path -- the access path resolves the window capability instead, and this value exists so the mint can inherit rather than be argued. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_bb_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_bb_out;
        mov rax, [rax + 8];
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_bb_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_bar_len : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the window length the probe will grant the capability. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_bl_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_bl_out;
        mov rax, [rax + 16];
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_bl_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_window : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the cap slot holding this controller's KIND_OP_REGION window, or 0 when unbound. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_win_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_win_out;
        mov rax, [rax + 24];
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_win_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_bus_row : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the KIND_I2C_BUS row this controller backs, or LPSS_I2C_NO_BUS_ROW. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_br_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_br_none;
        mov rax, [rax + 32];
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_br_none:
        mov rax, 0xFFFF;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_state : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the controller's bring-up state. LPSS_I2C_STATE_FREE for an out-of-range index or a free row, which is the honest answer: nothing is there. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_st_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_st_free;
        mov rax, [rax + 40];
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_st_free:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_clk : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the ic_clk frequency this controller was brought up against, 0 before bring-up. Recorded rather than recomputed because it is the one input to the divider that no register reports, so 'which clock did we assume' must be answerable after the fact. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_ck_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_ck_out;
        mov rax, [rax + 48];
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_ck_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_wedged : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): 1 iff the bus behind this controller has been latched wedged. Checked at the head of every transfer so a held bus fails fast with one code instead of burning a full timeout budget per call -- a wedged bus with N drivers on it would otherwise convert one stuck peripheral into N stalled callers. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_wg_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_wg_out;
        mov rax, [rax + 56];
        and rax, 0xFF;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_wg_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_ctrl_recover_fails : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): consecutive failed recovery attempts. Reset to zero by any successful recovery; at LPSS_I2C_RECOVER_MAX_FAILS the wedged latch is set. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_rf_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_rf_out;
        mov rax, [rax + 56];
        shr rax, 8;
        and rax, 0xFF;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_rf_out:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Mutators. The only writers of a live row outside the probe itself.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_set_state : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64, state: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): record a bring-up state transition. Exported rather than inlined at the call sites in dw_init.o and dw_xfer.o because tools/build.sh forbids those objects from relocating against _lpss_i2c_ctrl at all -- the same device that gives the bus row's live-device count exactly two mutators. Refuses a state above LPSS_I2C_STATE_REJECTED so a garbled value cannot make a controller look READY. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_ss_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        mov rbx, rsi;
        cmp rbx, 5;
        ja  lpi_ss_bad;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_ss_bad;
        mov [rax + 40], rbx;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_ss_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF4A;             // LPSS_I2C_STATE_BAD
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_set_clk : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64, clk_hz: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): record the ic_clk the controller was brought up against. No validation here; dw_i2c_bringup validates against the closed domain before it calls this, and duplicating the check would put the domain in two places. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_sc_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        mov rbx, rsi;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_sc_bad;
        mov [rax + 48], rbx;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_sc_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5E;             // LPSS_I2C_PROBE_BAD_IDX
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_note_recover_ok : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): a recovery succeeded -- clear the consecutive-failure counter, leaving the wedged latch alone. The latch is deliberately NOT cleared here: once a bus has been declared wedged, the exit is the tier-2 recovery that this milestone defers (see dw_xfer.pdx), and letting a lucky recovery silently un-wedge it would hide a bus that is failing intermittently behind one that recovered once. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_ro_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_ro_bad;
        mov r10, [rax + 56];
        mov r11, 0xFF;
        and r10, r11;                    // keep wedged, drop fails
        mov [rax + 56], r10;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_ro_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5E;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_note_recover_fail : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): a recovery failed. Increments the consecutive-failure counter and, at LPSS_I2C_RECOVER_MAX_FAILS, LATCHES the wedged bit. Returns the new failure count so the caller can report which attempt latched it. The counter saturates at 255 rather than wrapping, because a wrapped count would clear the latch condition and let a permanently held bus be retried forever. Three attempts rather than one: a single failed abort can be a transient arbitration loss against a peer master, and declaring the bus dead on the first one would take a recoverable machine down. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_rl_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_rl_bad;

        mov r10, [rax + 56];
        mov r11, r10;
        shr r11, 8;
        and r11, 0xFF;
        cmp r11, 0xFF;
        jae lpi_rl_sat;
        add r11, 1;
      lpi_rl_sat:
        mov r9, r10;
        and r9, 0xFF;                    // wedged bits
        cmp r11, 3;                      // LPSS_I2C_RECOVER_MAX_FAILS
        jb  lpi_rl_store;
        mov r9, 1;                       // latch wedged
      lpi_rl_store:
        mov r10, r11;
        shl r10, 8;
        or  r10, r9;
        mov [rax + 56], r10;
        mov rax, r11;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      lpi_rl_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5E;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let lpss_i2c_unwedge : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): clear the wedged latch and the failure counter. THE ONLY EXIT FROM THE WEDGED STATE, and it exists so that state is a handled one rather than a terminal one. Its intended caller is the tier-2 bus recovery R30.M6 will build once KIND_GPIO_LINE can pulse the SCL pad -- freeing a peripheral that latched SDA low is a pin operation, not a controller operation, and this driver has no pin authority. Refuses LPSS_I2C_NOT_WEDGED on a controller that was never latched, so a caller cannot use it as a general failure-counter reset and thereby make the three-strike budget unreachable. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_uw_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_uw_bad;
        mov r10, [rax + 56];
        mov r11, r10;
        and r11, 0xFF;
        cmp r11, 0;
        je  lpi_uw_notwedged;
        xor r10, r10;
        mov [rax + 56], r10;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_uw_notwedged:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF4D;             // LPSS_I2C_NOT_WEDGED
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_uw_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5E;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_attach_bus(idx, bus_row) -> rc
  //
  // Join a probed controller to the KIND_I2C_BUS row that represents it.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_attach_bus : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64, bus_row: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): record which KIND_I2C_BUS row this controller backs. This is the join the transfer path walks in the other direction -- slave capability, to its bus row, to the controller that owns the wire -- and it is the ONLY such join, so a slave capability can never reach a controller its bus does not name. Refuses a bus_row >= I2C_BUS_MAX (8) and refuses to overwrite an existing attachment (LPSS_I2C_BUS_ATTACH_ALREADY): re-pointing a live controller at a different bus row would silently redirect every slave capability derived from the old one, which is the same class of confusion as a stale IC_TAR and is refused for the same reason. Detaching is not offered; the bus row's own revoke cascade is what ends the relationship. One push. Labels prefixed lpi_ab_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        mov rbx, rsi;
        cmp rbx, 8;
        jae lpi_ab_badrow;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_ab_badidx;
        mov r10, [rax + 32];
        mov r11, 0xFFFF;
        cmp r10, r11;
        jne lpi_ab_already;
        mov [rax + 32], rbx;
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_ab_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF51;             // LPSS_I2C_BUS_ATTACH_ALREADY
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_ab_badrow:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF52;             // LPSS_I2C_BUS_ATTACH_BAD_ROW
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_ab_badidx:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5E;             // LPSS_I2C_PROBE_BAD_IDX
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_find_by_bus_row(bus_row) -> idx | LPSS_I2C_NOT_FOUND
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_find_by_bus_row : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (bus_row: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): the controller index backing a given KIND_I2C_BUS row, or LPSS_I2C_NOT_FOUND. Linear over eight rows, which is small, and indexless because an index would be a second place the mapping lives. LPSS_I2C_NO_BUS_ROW is refused up front rather than matched: eight unattached controllers all carry it, and returning the first would hand a transfer to an arbitrary controller. Leaf. Labels prefixed lpi_fb_.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 0xFFFF;
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  lpi_fb_miss;

        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_ctrl];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      lpi_fb_next:
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jae lpi_fb_miss;
        mov rax, rcx;
        shl rax, 6;
        add rax, r10;
        mov r9, [rax + 0];
        and r9, 0xFF;
        cmp r9, 0;
        je  lpi_fb_bump;
        mov r9, [rax + 32];
        cmp r9, rdi;
        je  lpi_fb_hit;
      lpi_fb_bump:
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp lpi_fb_next;
      lpi_fb_hit:
        mov rax, rcx;
        ret;
      lpi_fb_miss:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_bar0(seg, bus, dev, fn, out) -> rc
  //
  // BAR0 discovery with the memory decoder correctly disabled across the
  // sizing write. `out` is a 32-byte bar_info buffer.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_bar0 : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} =
    fn (seg: u64, bus: u64, dev: u64, fnid: u64, out: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): size and decode BAR0 with the memory decoder disabled across the sizing write. Sequence: read COMMAND (config +0x04) and save it; write it back with bit 1 (memory space enable) cleared; pci_bar_size(seg,bus,dev,fn,0); read BAR0 low (+0x10) and high (+0x14); RESTORE the original COMMAND word; then decode. The disable is not ceremony -- pci_bar_size works by writing all-ones into the BAR, and a decoder left enabled across that write has the function claiming a window at 0xFFFFF000 for its duration, on top of whatever else is up there. The restore happens BEFORE any refusal path so a rejected BAR cannot leave the decoder off, which would take the device away from firmware as well as from us. The high dword is read unconditionally; pci_bar_decode ignores it unless bits [2:1] of the low dword say 64-bit, and an absent upper dword merely reads the next register. Refusals: size 0 (absent BAR), kind not MEM32/MEM64 (an I/O BAR is not a register window), or decoded length below DW_REG_WINDOW_BYTES. The length check is a refusal and not a clamp: a window shorter than the register bank means the BAR was misread or the part is not what we think, and both deserve to stop here rather than produce a capability that covers half a controller. Six-push callee-save prologue (rbx, rbp, r12-r15) = 48 B; entry rsp%16 == 8 -> 56; sub rsp, 24 -> 80, %16 == 0 at every nested call. Scratch: [rsp+0] size, [rsp+8] bar_lo, [rsp+16] bar_hi. Labels prefixed lpi_bar_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 24;

        mov rbx, rdi;                    // seg
        mov rbp, rsi;                    // bus
        mov r12, rdx;                    // dev
        mov r13, rcx;                    // fn
        mov r14, r8;                     // out

        // --- read COMMAND, save, clear memory-space enable ---
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, r12;
        mov rcx, r13;
        mov r8,  0x04;
        call pci_config_read_u32;
        mov r15, rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, r12;
        mov rcx, r13;
        mov r8,  0x04;
        mov r9,  r15;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFD;
        and r9,  r10;
        call pci_config_write_u32;

        // --- size BAR0 ---
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, r12;
        mov rcx, r13;
        mov r8,  0;
        call pci_bar_size;
        mov [rsp + 0], rax;

        // --- read BAR0 low / high ---
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, r12;
        mov rcx, r13;
        mov r8,  0x10;
        call pci_config_read_u32;
        mov [rsp + 8], rax;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, r12;
        mov rcx, r13;
        mov r8,  0x14;
        call pci_config_read_u32;
        mov [rsp + 16], rax;

        // --- restore COMMAND, unconditionally, before any refusal ---
        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, r12;
        mov rcx, r13;
        mov r8,  0x04;
        mov r9,  r15;
        call pci_config_write_u32;

        mov rax, [rsp + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_bar_none;

        // --- decode into the caller's 32-byte bar_info ---
        mov rdi, [rsp + 8];
        mov rsi, [rsp + 16];
        mov rdx, [rsp + 0];
        mov rcx, r14;
        call pci_bar_decode;

        // kind must be MEM32 (2) or MEM64 (3)
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14 + 16];
        cmp rax, 2;
        jb  lpi_bar_none;
        cmp rax, 3;
        ja  lpi_bar_none;

        // decoded length must cover the register bank
        mov rax, [r14 + 8];
        mov r10, 0x1000;                 // DW_REG_WINDOW_BYTES
        cmp rax, r10;
        jb  lpi_bar_none;

        xor rax, rax;
        add rsp, 24;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      lpi_bar_none:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5D;             // LPSS_I2C_PROBE_NO_BAR
        add rsp, 24;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_record(bdf_key, intel, bar_pa, bar_len) -> idx | error
  //
  // THE ROW ALLOCATOR — the only function that brings a controller row
  // into existence, and therefore the only place the confinement
  // argument has to hold.
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // It is exported rather than being private to `lpss_i2c_probe`, and
  // that deserves justifying rather than assuming, because "the table
  // has one writer object" is a claim `tools/build.sh` enforces.
  //
  // The claim is unaffected. A controller row confers NO REACH. It
  // records a BDF, a measured extent and some bookkeeping; nothing in
  // the access path reads `bar0_pa`. The only thing that turns an
  // extent into the ability to touch memory is
  // `lpss_i2c_bind_window`, which mints a `KIND_OP_REGION` root and is
  // gated on a `KIND_MEMORY` capability carrying `RIGHT_MINT`. A caller
  // who can reach that gate could call `opregion_cap_mint_root`
  // directly with any base it liked, so being able to write a row adds
  // nothing to what it could already do.
  //
  // What the export buys is that the boot witness allocates rows
  // through the SAME allocator the probe uses, rather than through a
  // fixture-only back door that could drift from it. A fixture that
  // builds rows a different way tests a shape the machine never has.

  pub let lpss_i2c_record : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (bdf_key: u64, intel: u64, bar_pa: u64, bar_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): allocate one controller row from a bdf_pack key, the Intel-wrapper flag, and a measured BAR extent. Decomposes the key (fn[7:0], dev[15:8], bus[23:16], seg[39:24]) into the row header's own field order, which differs because the header also carries in_use and the wrapper flag; keeping the two encodings distinct and converting once here is better than a header that is 'almost' a bdf key and is read as one by mistake. Refuses a zero-length extent (PROBE_NO_BAR) -- a row whose window is empty would mint a capability covering nothing and then refuse every register access with a range error that names the wrong problem. Refuses past LPSS_I2C_MAX_CTRL (PROBE_ENOSPC). Rows are initialised to CANDIDATE with no window, no bus and no clock, because every one of those is a later, separately-gated step. Bumps _lpss_i2c_count. Leaf; no nested calls. Labels prefixed lpi_rec_.",
      block: {
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je  lpi_rec_nobar;

        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_count];
        mov r11, [r10 + 0];
        cmp r11, 8;                      // LPSS_I2C_MAX_CTRL
        jae lpi_rec_enospc;

        mov rax, r11;
        shl rax, 6;
        lea r9, [rip + _lpss_i2c_ctrl];
        add r9, rax;

        // hdr = in_use | bus<<8 | dev<<16 | fn<<24 | seg<<32 | intel<<48
        mov rax, 1;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shr r8, 16;
        and r8, 0xFF;
        shl r8, 8;                       // bus
        or  rax, r8;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shr r8, 8;
        and r8, 0xFF;
        shl r8, 16;                      // dev
        or  rax, r8;
        mov r8, rdi;
        and r8, 0xFF;
        shl r8, 24;                      // fn
        or  rax, r8;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shr r8, 24;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and r8, r10;
        shl r8, 32;                      // seg
        or  rax, r8;
        mov r8, rsi;
        and r8, 0xFF;
        shl r8, 48;                      // intel wrapper
        or  rax, r8;
        mov [r9 + 0], rax;

        mov [r9 + 8],  rdx;              // bar0_pa
        mov [r9 + 16], rcx;              // bar0_len
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r9 + 24], rax;              // window_slot
        mov rax, 0xFFFF;
        mov [r9 + 32], rax;              // bus_row = NO_BUS_ROW
        mov rax, 1;                      // STATE_CANDIDATE
        mov [r9 + 40], rax;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r9 + 48], rax;              // ic_clk_hz
        mov [r9 + 56], rax;              // health

        mov rax, r11;
        add r11, 1;
        lea r10, [rip + _lpss_i2c_count];
        mov [r10 + 0], r11;
        ret;

      lpi_rec_nobar:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5D;             // LPSS_I2C_PROBE_NO_BAR
        ret;
      lpi_rec_enospc:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5F;             // LPSS_I2C_PROBE_ENOSPC
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_probe() -> number of candidate controllers recorded
  //
  // Stage 1 of identification. No MMIO, no capability, no bring-up: this
  // walks the enumerator's published device list, keeps the class
  // candidates, measures BAR0, and stops.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_probe : () -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): walk _pci_devices (32-byte records, class_code at +6, subclass at +7, vendor at +8, seg/bus/dev/fn at +0/+2/+3/+4) and record every CLASS CANDIDATE for a DesignWare I2C core. The filter is (0x0C, 0x80) or (0x11, 0x80) and carries NO device-id table -- see the file header for why a device-id table's failure mode (a present, healthy, unprobed controller and a silent log) is the worst one available. Vendor 0x8086 is recorded as the intel_wrapper bit, not used as a filter, so a non-Intel DesignWare instance on PCI is still a candidate; it merely skips the Intel private-space reset release. Each candidate's BAR0 is measured through lpss_i2c_bar0 (decoder disabled across the sizing write) and a BAR that is absent, is I/O, or is under 4 KiB drops the candidate rather than recording a window nobody can use. Overflow past LPSS_I2C_MAX_CTRL drops silently but observably: the emitted count is the number RECORDED, and a machine with nine LPSS controllers would show eight. Rows are left in LPSS_I2C_STATE_CANDIDATE with no window and no bus; binding a window is a separate, capability-bearing step. Six-push prologue + sub rsp, 40 (32-byte bar_info at [rsp+0], scratch count at [rsp+32]); entry rsp%16 == 8, 48 pushed -> 56, sub 40 -> 96, %16 == 0. Labels prefixed lpi_pr_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push rbp;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;
        sub rsp, 40;

        call lpss_i2c_table_reset;

        xor r13, r13;                    // j = recorded
        lea r10, [rip + _pci_device_count];
        mov r12, [r10 + 0];
        xor rbx, rbx;                    // i

      lpi_pr_next:
        cmp rbx, r12;
        jae lpi_pr_done;

        mov rax, rbx;
        shl rax, 5;
        lea r14, [rip + _pci_devices];
        add r14, rax;

        // class / subclass candidate filter
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14 + 7];
        cmp rax, 0x80;                   // LPSS_SUBCLASS_OTHER
        jne lpi_pr_bump;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [r14 + 6];
        cmp rax, 0x0C;
        je  lpi_pr_cand;
        cmp rax, 0x11;
        jne lpi_pr_bump;

      lpi_pr_cand:
        cmp r13, 8;                      // LPSS_I2C_MAX_CTRL
        jae lpi_pr_bump;

        // BAR0 discovery
        xor rdi, rdi;
        mov_w rdi, [r14 + 0];            // seg
        xor rsi, rsi;
        mov_b rsi, [r14 + 2];            // bus
        xor rdx, rdx;
        mov_b rdx, [r14 + 3];            // dev
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [r14 + 4];            // fn
        mov r8, rsp;                     // bar_info
        call lpss_i2c_bar0;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne lpi_pr_bump;

        // ---- record the row through the shared allocator ----
        xor rdi, rdi;
        mov_w rdi, [r14 + 0];            // seg
        xor rsi, rsi;
        mov_b rsi, [r14 + 2];            // bus
        xor rdx, rdx;
        mov_b rdx, [r14 + 3];            // dev
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [r14 + 4];            // fn
        call bdf_pack;
        mov r15, rax;                    // bdf key

        xor rbp, rbp;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_w rax, [r14 + 8];            // vendor
        mov r10, 0x8086;
        cmp rax, r10;
        jne lpi_pr_nointel;
        mov rbp, 1;
      lpi_pr_nointel:

        mov rdi, r15;
        mov rsi, rbp;
        mov rdx, [rsp + 0];              // bar_info.base_pa
        mov rcx, [rsp + 8];              // bar_info.size
        call lpss_i2c_record;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFF30;
        cmp rax, r10;
        jae lpi_pr_bump;

        add r13, 1;

      lpi_pr_bump:
        add rbx, 1;
        jmp lpi_pr_next;

      lpi_pr_done:
        // The count is maintained by lpss_i2c_record, which is the sole
        // allocator; r13 is only this loop's tally of accepted rows and
        // is used for the fingerprint. Storing it here as well would put
        // the count in two places, which is how the two come to differ.
        mov [rsp + 32], r13;

        mov rdi, 3;                      // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
        lea rdx, [rip + lpi_tag_probe];
        lea rcx, [rip + lpi_k_n];
        mov r8, r13;
        call klog_s1_d1;

        mov rax, [rsp + 32];
        add rsp, 40;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbp;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_bind_window(idx, mem_parent_slot, win_slot, rights) -> rc
  //
  // THE BAR-TO-CAPABILITY STEP. Mints a KIND_OP_REGION root over the
  // caller's KIND_MEMORY authority, covering exactly the extent the
  // probe measured, and records the slot.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_bind_window : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (idx: u64, mem_parent_slot: u64, win_slot: u64, rights: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): turn a measured BAR into a capability. Calls opregion_cap_mint_root(win_slot, mem_parent_slot, rights, OPREG_SPACE_PCI_BAR = 0x06, base, len) where BASE AND LEN ARE THE ROW'S OWN -- inherited from what the probe measured, not arguments. That is the whole point: if the caller supplied the base, a holder of memory authority could mint itself a window over any physical address and call it an I2C controller, which is precisely the class of bug KIND_OP_REGION exists to remove, reintroduced by its own client. Space 0x06 is memory-like per opregion_space_base_kind, so the mint demands a KIND_MEMORY (4) parent carrying RIGHT_MINT and refuses anything else -- an I/O-port holder cannot manufacture register reach. The op-region refusal code is mapped to LPSS_I2C_WIN_BAD_PARENT rather than passed through, because a caller here is debugging a driver wiring problem and wants to know 'the window mint was refused', with the op-region band's own code already in the audit record the mint wrapper emitted. Refuses LPSS_I2C_WIN_ALREADY on a row that already has a window: re-binding would leave the previous capability live and pointing at the same registers, i.e. two authorities for one controller with no revoke relationship between them. On success the state advances CANDIDATE -> WINDOWED. Five-push prologue (rbx, r12-r15) leaves rsp%16 == 0 at every nested call. Labels prefixed lpi_bw_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // idx
        mov r13, rsi;                    // mem parent slot
        mov r14, rdx;                    // window slot
        mov r15, rcx;                    // rights

        mov rdi, r12;
        call lpss_i2c_row;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  lpi_bw_badidx;
        mov rbx, rax;

        mov rax, [rbx + 24];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne lpi_bw_already;

        mov rdi, r14;                    // subject slot
        mov rsi, r13;                    // parent (KIND_MEMORY)
        mov rdx, r15;                    // rights
        mov rcx, 0x06;                   // OPREG_SPACE_PCI_BAR
        mov r8,  [rbx + 8];              // base   — INHERITED
        mov r9,  [rbx + 16];             // len    — INHERITED
        call opregion_cap_mint_root;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne lpi_bw_refused;

        mov [rbx + 24], r14;
        mov rax, 2;                      // STATE_WINDOWED
        mov [rbx + 40], rax;

        xor rax, rax;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      lpi_bw_refused:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5B;             // LPSS_I2C_WIN_BAD_PARENT
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_bw_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5A;             // LPSS_I2C_WIN_ALREADY
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      lpi_bw_badidx:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF5E;             // LPSS_I2C_PROBE_BAD_IDX
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // lpss_i2c_reject(idx) -> rc
  //
  // Stage-2 failure. LOUD by construction.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let lpss_i2c_reject : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{} =
    fn (idx: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M5-003 (#1072): move a candidate to LPSS_I2C_STATE_REJECTED and EMIT A LOG LINE carrying its BDF. The emission is the point and is why this is a function rather than a state store: a class-matched device that failed the IC_COMP_TYPE confirmation is the single most informative event this driver can produce on a machine nobody has tried before -- it says 'something is at this address and it is not the core we expected', which is the sentence that turns an unexplained dead touchpad into a one-line bug report. A silent rejection would be indistinguishable from never having probed. LEVEL_ERROR rather than INFO because a rejected candidate means a device the platform exposed is now unusable. Three pushes (rbx, r12, r13) leave rsp%16 == 0 at both nested calls. Labels prefixed lpi_rj_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov rdi, r12;
        call lpss_i2c_ctrl_bdf;
        mov r13, rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        mov rsi, 5;                      // STATE_REJECTED
        call lpss_i2c_set_state;
        mov rbx, rax;

        mov rdi, 1;                      // LEVEL_ERROR
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_BOOT];
        lea rdx, [rip + lpi_tag_reject];
        lea rcx, [rip + lpi_k_bdf];
        mov r8, r13;
        call klog_s1_x1;

        mov rax, rbx;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
